Chapter 2: Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 Probability and Decision-Theoretic Setup

Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}) be a complete probability space. The unknown state of nature is parameterised by \theta \in \Theta, where \Theta is a measurable space. Observed data is denoted \mathcal{D}, and posterior uncertainty is captured by the conditional distribution p(\theta \mid \mathcal{D}). The admissible decision space \mathcal{A} is a compact subset of a metric space equipped with metric d.

Assumption A1 (Compactness). \mathcal{A} is compact.

Assumption A2 (Continuity). The loss function L: \mathcal{A} \times \Theta \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is continuous in a for each fixed \theta.

Assumption A3 (Boundedness). There exists a constant B < \infty such that 0 \le L(a,\theta) \le B for all a \in \mathcal{A} and \theta \in \Theta.

Assumption A4 (Posterior existence). The posterior distribution p(\theta \mid \mathcal{D}) exists and is absolutely continuous with respect to a fixed prior measure.

2.2 Bayes Risk and Optimal Actions

Definition 2.1 (Bayes Risk). The Bayes risk of action a given data \mathcal{D} is

R(a) = \mathbb{E}_{\theta \sim p(\theta \mid \mathcal{D})}[L(a,\theta)] = \int_{\Theta} L(a,\theta) \, p(\theta \mid \mathcal{D}) \, d\theta.

Theorem 2.1 (Existence of Bayes-Optimal Actions). Under Assumptions A1–A4, there exists at least one Bayes-optimal action a^* \in \arg\min_{a \in \mathcal{A}} R(a).

Proof. Because L(\cdot,\theta) is continuous on the compact set \mathcal{A} and uniformly bounded by B, the Dominated Convergence Theorem implies that R(\cdot) is continuous on \mathcal{A}. The Weierstrass Extreme Value Theorem guarantees that a continuous function on a compact set attains its minimum. \square

Remark (Uniqueness). Existence is guaranteed; uniqueness is not. If \mathcal{A} is convex and R is strictly convex, the minimiser is unique. For discrete policy menus, multiple optimal actions may exist; in such cases a^* denotes any element of the \arg\min set.

2.3 Posterior Regret and Confidence

Definition 2.2 (Posterior Regret). For any action a \in \mathcal{A}, the posterior regret is

\rho(a) = R(a) - R(a^*) \ge 0,
\]  

where a^* is any Bayes-optimal action.

Definition 2.3 (Confidence Operator). Let \rho_{\max} = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \rho(a). The confidence assigned to action a is defined as

\mathcal{C}(a) = 
\begin{cases}
1 - \dfrac{\rho(a)}{\rho_{\max}}, & \text{if } \rho_{\max} > 0, \\[1em]
1, & \text{if } \rho_{\max} = 0.
\end{cases}

Proposition 2.1 (Boundedness of Confidence). For every a \in \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{C}(a) \in [0,1].

Proof. If \rho_{\max} > 0, then 0 \le \rho(a) \le \rho_{\max} implies 0 \le \rho(a)/\rho_{\max} \le 1, so \mathcal{C}(a) \in [0,1]. If \rho_{\max} = 0, then by definition \mathcal{C}(a) = 1 \in [0,1]. \square

Interpretation. A confidence value of \mathcal{C}(a) = 0.95 means that the action a achieves at least 95% of the best possible expected performance under the assumed model. It does not mean there is a 95% probability that a is the true optimal action.

