Chapter 4: Confidence-Indexed Hierarchical Probabilistic Consequence Graphs

4.1 Definition as a Marked Galton–Watson Tree

We model the propagation of consequences as a rooted, directed tree. Feedback loops in the actual temporal dynamics are handled by time-unrolling: distinct temporal copies of a feedback-linked variable are represented as separate nodes connected by edges, thereby preserving the tree structure. The fundamental object is a marked Galton–Watson process.

To ensure rigorous handling of random node identities, we adopt the Ulam–Harris labelling convention. The set of all possible node labels is

V = \bigcup_{k=0}^\infty \mathbb{N}_{>0}^k,
\]  

with the empty sequence \varnothing denoting the root. A child of a node v = (i_1, \dots, i_k) is labelled by concatenating an additional positive integer, v' = (i_1, \dots, i_k, j) for some j \in \mathbb{N}_{>0}. The random tree is a random subset \mathcal{V} \subseteq V; a node v is said to be realised if the corresponding sequence of branching choices occurs. The unconditional realisation probability P(v) is then the probability that v \in \mathcal{V}.

Definition 4.1 (CI‑HPCG). A confidence-indexed hierarchical probabilistic consequence graph is a tuple

\mathcal{G} = (V, E, P, I, C, \tau, a_0),
\]  

where:

· V is the universal set of possible node labels (Ulam–Harris labels), forming the ambient tree.
· E \subset V \times V is the set of parent–child edges consistent with the tree structure: (v, v') \in E if and only if v' extends v by one coordinate.
· P : V \to [0,1] assigns to each node its unconditional realisation probability. For the root, P(\varnothing) = 1; for any non-root node v with parent \pi(v), P(v) = P(\pi(v)) \cdot p(v \mid \pi(v)), where p(v \mid \pi(v)) is the conditional probability that v occurs given its parent is realised.
· I : V \to \mathbb{R}^n assigns an impact vector to each node.
· C : V \to [0,1] assigns a confidence value to each node, obtained by applying the confidence operator of Definition 2.3 to the local sub-decision that generates the node. At pure chance nodes, where no decision is taken, we set C(v) \equiv 1.
· \tau : V \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} assigns a time horizon (or temporal distance from the root) to each node.
· a_0 \in \mathcal{A} is the initial decision at the root.

4.2 Branching Structure and Update Operator

Definition 4.2 (Branching Kernel). For each realised node v \in \mathcal{V}, a branching kernel \kappa generates a random finite set of children. The number of children N_v is a random variable with bounded expectation.

Assumption A6 (Bounded Branching). There exists a constant \mu < \infty such that for every node v,

\mathbb{E}[N_v \mid \text{parent realised}] \le \mu.
\]  

Equality holds in the homogeneous case where the offspring distribution is identical for all nodes with mean exactly \mu.

Thus, the random tree \mathcal{V} is a Galton–Watson tree with marks (I(v), C(v), \tau(v)) \in \mathbb{R}^n \times [0,1] \times \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}.

Definition 4.3 (Update Operator). The operator \mathcal{T} : \mathcal{G}_t \to \mathcal{G}_{t+\Delta} advances the graph in time by performing two operations:
(1) Belief update: For existing nodes, the probabilities P(v), impacts I(v), and confidences C(v) are updated via Bayes' rule using newly acquired evidence.
(2) Frontier expansion: New children are sampled from the current frontier nodes according to the branching kernel \kappa.

4.3 The Discounted Consequence Functional

Definition 4.4 (Discounted Consequence Functional). For a discount rate \lambda > 0 and a norm \|\cdot\| on \mathbb{R}^n, the expected total discounted impact of the initial decision a_0 is

J(a_0) = \mathbb{E}\Biggl[ \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}} \|I(v)\| \, e^{-\lambda \tau(v)} \Biggr]
= \sum_{v \in V} P(v) \cdot \|I(v)\| \cdot e^{-\lambda \tau(v)}.
\]  

The second equality follows from the linearity of expectation, using the fact that the indicator of node realisation has expectation P(v). Note that the sum runs over the universal set V of all possible nodes, with P(v)=0 for unrealised nodes.

Theorem 4.1 (Expected Population Size at Depth k). Under Assumption A6, the expected number of realised nodes at depth k \ge 0 satisfies

\mathbb{E}[|V_k|] \le \mu^k,
\]  

with equality when branching is homogeneous with mean exactly \mu.

Proof. The claim follows by induction on depth, using the law of total expectation and the bounded mean offspring condition. \square

Theorem 4.2 (Absolute Convergence of Expected Discounted Impact). Suppose the following hold:
(i) There exists a uniform bound M < \infty such that \|I(v)\| \le M for all v \in V.
(ii) There exist constants c > 0 and \lambda > 0 such that for every node v at depth k, \tau(v) \ge c \cdot k.
(iii) The subcriticality condition

\mu \, e^{-\lambda c} < 1
\]  

is satisfied.
Then the series defining J(a_0) converges absolutely, and

J(a_0) \le \frac{M}{1 - \mu e^{-\lambda c}} < \infty.
\]  

Moreover, the random total discounted impact S = \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}} \|I(v)\| e^{-\lambda \tau(v)} is finite almost surely, and \mathbb{E}[S] = J(a_0).

Proof. Decompose the sum by depth: V_k = \{v \in V : \text{depth}(v) = k\}. For any v \in V_k, P(v) \le 1, \|I(v)\| \le M, and \tau(v) \ge c k. Hence the contribution of depth k to J(a_0) is bounded by

\sum_{v \in V_k} P(v) \cdot M \cdot e^{-\lambda c k} = M e^{-\lambda c k} \, \mathbb{E}[|V_k|] \le M (\mu e^{-\lambda c})^k,
\]  

where we used Theorem 4.1 to bound \mathbb{E}[|V_k|] \le \mu^k. Summing over all depths,

J(a_0) \le \sum_{k=0}^\infty M (\mu e^{-\lambda c})^k = \frac{M}{1 - \mu e^{-\lambda c}} < \infty,
\]  

since \mu e^{-\lambda c} < 1 by assumption. This establishes absolute convergence of the expected series. For the random sum S, Fubini's theorem (or Tonelli's theorem, as the terms are non-negative) gives \mathbb{E}[S] = J(a_0) < \infty, which implies S < \infty almost surely. \square

Interpretation. The condition \mu e^{-\lambda c} < 1 requires that the expected consequence multiplication factor \mu be outrun by the exponential discounting e^{-\lambda c} per generation. When this fails, the expected total discounted impact diverges, signalling that the decision problem is inherently unstable under the chosen time‑scale and discount rate. In such cases, the model's inputs (\mu, \lambda, c) must be re-examined.

4.4 Conditional Fractal Characterisation

A particularly structured special case arises when the consequence graph exhibits strict self-similarity in impacts and timing. In this subcase, a fractal dimension can be assigned.

Definition 4.5 (Fractal Consequence Graph). The CI‑HPCG is said to be self-similar if there exist constants r \in (0,1) and c > 0 such that for every node v and each of its children v',

\|I(v')\| = r \cdot \|I(v)\|, \qquad \tau(v') = \tau(v) + c,
\]  

and the branching is regular with exactly \mu children per node (deterministically or almost surely). The tuple (\mu, r, c) fully parameterises the propagation.

Theorem 4.3 (Fractal Dimension Under Self-Similarity). Under the self-similarity hypothesis, if the sets of scaled impact magnitudes satisfy the open set condition for the iterated function system (IFS) consisting of \mu similarities each of ratio r, then the Hausdorff dimension of the limit set of impact magnitudes (conditional on non-extinction) is

D_b = \frac{\ln \mu}{\ln(1/r)}.

Proof sketch. The branching and scaling rule defines an IFS of \mu contracting similarities with contraction ratio r. By standard results in fractal geometry (Falconer, 2003), the similarity dimension D_b satisfies the Moran equation \mu \cdot r^{D_b} = 1, yielding D_b = \ln \mu / \ln(1/r). Under the open set condition, the similarity dimension equals the Hausdorff dimension of the attractor of the IFS. The condition that temporal increments are positive and constant ensures that distinct branches correspond to separated copies, supporting the open set condition. \square

Note. The term “Fractal Consequence Graph” is reserved strictly for this self-similar subcase. In general, the CI‑HPCG does not possess a well-defined fractal dimension, and no such claim is made.

