Chapter 5: Toward a Coupled Dynamical System

5.1 The Natural Coupling

A fully integrated theory would allow the branching behaviour of the consequence graph to depend on the confidence attached to each decision node. Intuitively, low confidence in a decision should prompt consideration of more alternative consequence branches (wider exploration), while high confidence might allow a narrower, more focused propagation. Formally, this suggests that the offspring distribution at a decision node v is a function of its local confidence mark C(v).

5.2 Branching Processes in Random Environment (BPRE)

When the offspring distribution depends on an external or internally generated stochastic process, the resulting tree is no longer a simple Galton–Watson process but a branching process in a random environment (BPRE). Classical results for BPREs (Smith & Wilkinson, 1969; Athreya & Karlin, 1971) state that, under stationary and ergodic environmental sequences, the extinction probability is governed by the sign of the logarithmic mean:

\mathbb{E}[\ln m_0] < 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{almost sure extinction},
\]  

\mathbb{E}[\ln m_0] > 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{positive survival probability},
\]  

where m_k is the mean offspring number in generation k.

5.3 The Remaining Gap

Theorem 3.3 provides almost-sure convergence of the scalar confidence value at the root. This establishes a useful regularity property for the environmental process driving branching, but the full classification of the coupled process as a BPRE requires stronger conditions—such as stationarity or ergodicity of the confidence sequence—which have not been proved to follow from Bayesian updating alone. The coupling problem is therefore partially illuminated but not yet resolved.

5.4 A Simplified Global Coupling

A tractable intermediate model is obtained by linking the global confidence at the root to the mean branching factor of the entire tree.

Model 5.1 (Global Confidence–Branching Coupling). Let \mu_t = \mu_0 \cdot \phi(\mathcal{C}_t(a_0)), where \mu_0 is a base branching factor and \phi : [0,1] \to [\phi_{\min}, \phi_{\max}] is a decreasing function (so lower confidence increases branching).

Proposition 5.1 (Uniform Convergence Under Simplified Coupling). If \mu_0 \phi_{\max} e^{-\lambda c} < 1, then the expected discounted impact J_t(a_0) converges absolutely for all t, uniformly in t.

Proof. Since \mu_t \le \mu_0 \phi_{\max} for all t, the uniform bound of Theorem 4.2 applies with \mu replaced by \mu_0 \phi_{\max}. The subcriticality condition is exactly the one stated. \square

5.5 Full Coupling: Open Problem

The general case, where each decision node’s offspring distribution depends on its own local confidence mark C(v), with all marks evolving dynamically via Bayesian updating as the graph expands, constitutes a fully coupled, node-indexed BPRE. Characterising the long-term behaviour (extinction, convergence, growth rates) of such a process remains an open problem and is listed as a Tier 1 gap in the next chapter.

