Chapter 6: Gaps and Open Problems

This chapter provides a complete, tiered inventory of what has been proved, what is conditional on additional assumptions, and what remains genuinely open. All gaps identified in earlier drafts are mapped to their current status.

6.1 Resolved Gaps

The following problems, present in preliminary versions, are now closed by theorems within this dissertation.

· Gap 1 – Ad hoc confidence heuristic. Replaced by the decision-theoretic operator \mathcal{C}(a) = 1 - \rho(a)/\rho_{\max} (Definition 2.3, Proposition 2.1), anchored to posterior regret.
· Gap 2 – Circular definition of suboptimality probability. Resolved by defining the regret \rho(a) and the \varepsilon-regret probability \mathbb{P}(\rho(a) > \varepsilon \mid \mathcal{D}), which is well-posed without circularity.
· Gap 3 – Convergence of the confidence process. Closed by Theorem 3.3, which shows almost-sure convergence of \mathcal{C}_t(a) under A1–A4 and A7, without requiring uniqueness or convexity.
· Gap 4 – Convergence of the consequence functional. Closed by Theorem 4.2 under the explicit subcriticality condition \mu e^{-\lambda c} < 1.
· Gap 5 – Unjustified fractal claim. The term “fractal” is now reserved for the self-similar subcase (Definition 4.5), where Theorem 4.3 provides a Hausdorff dimension via the Moran equation. The general case makes no fractal assertion.

6.2 Tier 1 Open Problems

These gaps circumscribe the limits of the current theory and represent significant challenges.

· Gap 6 – Stability of the recommended action without A8. Theorem 3.4 proves convergence of the optimal action under the quadratic growth condition A8. For discrete policy menus with tied optima or flat regions in R_\infty, A8 fails, and the recommended action may oscillate indefinitely even though the confidence value converges. The behaviour of a_t^* without A8 remains an open problem.
· Gap 7 – Model misspecification. Confidence \mathcal{C}(a) is coherent with respect to the assumed posterior p(\theta \mid \mathcal{D}), not calibrated to an external ground truth. A misspecified model can be confidently wrong. Potential mitigations (Bayesian model averaging, robust Bayes, imprecise probability) are discussed but not formally integrated.
· Gap 8 – Dependent-data finite-sample bounds. PAC‑Bayesian type bounds typically require i.i.d. or exchangeable data. National-scale time series violate these assumptions. Extensions using mixing conditions or martingale-based PAC‑Bayes bounds (Alquier, Seldin) are possible but not developed here.
· Gap 9 – Choice of time-discretisation \Delta. Acyclicity is enforced by time-unrolling with a chosen time-step \Delta. The effective propagation parameters c and \mu depend on this choice. No systematic procedure for selecting \Delta is provided.
· Gap 10 – Non-uniqueness of Bayes-optimal actions. When the \arg\min set contains more than one action, the confidence value at a node depends on the particular optimal action selected. A set-valued confidence (e.g., an interval based on the range of regrets) would be a natural extension but is not developed.
· Gap 11 – Unspecified branching kernel. The branching parameter \mu and the full offspring distribution are treated as exogenous inputs. No elicitation method or data-driven identification strategy is given.

6.3 Tier 2 Open Problems

These gaps weaken generality or practical applicability but are not fatal to the framework.

· Gap 12 – Fractal open set condition. Theorem 4.3 relies on strict self-similarity and the open set condition, which are unlikely in real policy settings. The diagnostic value of the fractal dimension is therefore limited.
· Gap 13 – Scalar confidence. Collapsing multidimensional uncertainty to a single scalar C(a) \in [0,1] may hide important tail risks. Distribution-valued confidence or risk-sensitive measures (e.g., CVaR-based confidence) represent future extensions.
· Gap 14 – Fully coupled dynamics (node-indexed BPRE). The coupling where each node’s offspring distribution depends on its local confidence C(v), with all marks evolving via Bayesian updating, leads to a branching process in a random environment indexed by the tree. Classical extinction criteria require ergodicity of the environment sequence, which has not been proved from Bayesian updating alone. The simplified global coupling of §5.4 provides a sufficient condition; the local coupling remains open.

---

Chapter 7: Conclusion and Future Work

7.1 Summary of Contributions

This dissertation has formalised recursive, uncertainty-aware decision propagation as the Confidence-Indexed Hierarchical Probabilistic Consequence Graph (CI‑HPCG) framework. The CI‑HPCG is a marked Galton–Watson tree whose nodes carry a decision-theoretically grounded confidence value derived from posterior regret. The central theoretical result is a precise separation between convergence of the confidence value (Theorem 3.3, unconditional under mild regularity) and convergence of the recommended action (Theorem 3.4, conditional on quadratic growth of the limiting risk). The discounted consequence functional is proved to converge absolutely under a checkable subcriticality condition (Theorem 4.2), and a conditional fractal characterisation is given for the self-similar subcase (Theorem 4.3). All assumptions are stated explicitly, and an honest tiered inventory of open problems distinguishes proven results from open challenges.

7.2 Future Work

· Direction 1: Weaken Assumption A8 using Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz growth conditions to obtain partial stability rates in non-quadratic settings.
· Direction 2: Extend the coupling of Chapter 5 toward a node-indexed BPRE, using Theorem 3.3 as a building block for environment regularity.
· Direction 3: Derive dependent-data PAC‑Bayes bounds to address Gap 8, enabling finite-sample confidence statements for time-series decision problems.
· Direction 4: Develop set-valued or distribution-valued confidence representations to capture tail risks and multidimensional uncertainty.
· Direction 5: Integrate formal causal identification so that edges E encode causal relationships rather than mere modelling convenience.
· Direction 6: Design approximation algorithms (e.g., Monte Carlo tree search, variational inference) with provable guarantees for evaluating J(a_0)—explicitly left as future computational work.


