Appendix B: Ulam–Harris Labelling and Formal Construction of the Consequence Graph

We provide a rigorous construction of the universal tree $V$ and the probability measure $P$ used in Definition 4.1.

B.1 The Universal Tree
Let $\mathbb{N}_{>0} = \{1,2,3,\dots\}$. The set of all finite sequences (including the empty sequence $\varnothing$) is

V := \bigcup_{k=0}^\infty \mathbb{N}_{>0}^k,

where $\mathbb{N}_{>0}^0 := \{\varnothing\}$. For $v = (i_1,\dots,i_k)\in V$, the depth of $v$ is $|v| = k$. The parent of a non-root node $v=(i_1,\dots,i_k)$ is $\pi(v) = (i_1,\dots,i_{k-1})$ (with the convention $\pi(\varnothing)$ undefined). The edge set $E$ consists of pairs $(v,\pi(v))$ oriented away from the root, or equivalently parent $\to$ child. This yields a rooted directed tree in which every node except $\varnothing$ has exactly one parent.

B.2 Branching Probabilities and Realisation
The branching kernel $\kappa$ is a mechanism that, for any realised node $v$, produces a random finite (possibly empty) set of children. In the Ulam–Harris framework, we can specify the randomness by assigning to each node $v\in V$ an independent random variable $X_v$ (e.g., the number of children) and then deterministically labelling the children as $(v,1), (v,2), \dots, (v,X_v)$. The process starts at the root with $X_\varnothing$, and recursively defines the realised tree $\mathcal{V}$ as the set of labels that are actually generated. Because the space of children labels is disjoint across nodes, this construction yields a well-defined random subset of $V$.

B.3 Unconditional Realisation Probability $P(v)$
For $v = (i_1,\dots,i_k)$, define $P(v)$ as the probability that the node $v$ is generated (i.e., $v\in\mathcal{V}$). This can be expressed recursively:

P(\varnothing) = 1,

P(v) = P(\pi(v)) \cdot \mathbb{P}(X_{\pi(v)} \ge i_k \mid \pi(v) \text{ realised}),

where we interpret the conditional probability of having at least $i_k$ children as $p(v\mid\pi(v))$. Under Assumption A6, these probabilities are well-defined and sum over all children to at most the mean $\mu$. In the general marked case, the marks $I(v), C(v), \tau(v)$ are also assigned at the time of node creation, possibly depending on the parent’s marks and the realisation of the child index.

B.4 The Discounted Consequence Functional
With the above, the expected sum in Definition 4.4 is exactly

J(a_0) = \sum_{v\in V} P(v)\, \|I(v)\|\, e^{-\lambda\tau(v)},

where $V$ is the universal (deterministic) set of all possible labels. The sum converges because $P(v)$ accounts for realisation. This framing makes the application of Fubini/Tonelli straightforward.

