Appendix C: Detailed Proof of Confidence Convergence (Theorem 3.3)

We expand the proof of Theorem 3.3, paying close attention to the handling of the degenerate case $\rho_{\max,\infty}=0$ and the almost-sure existence of a time after which $\rho_{\max,t}>0$.

Theorem 3.3. Under A1–A4 and A7, if $\rho_{\max,\infty} > 0$, then for every fixed $a\in\mathcal{A}$, $\mathcal{C}_t(a) \to \mathcal{C}_\infty(a)$ almost surely.

Proof.
Recall $\delta_t = \|R_t - R_\infty\|_\infty$, and $\delta_t\to0$ a.s. by Lemma 3.2. Define

m_t = \min_{a} R_t(a), \quad m_\infty = \min_{a} R_\infty(a), \quad
\rho_t(a) = R_t(a)-m_t, \quad \rho_\infty(a) = R_\infty(a)-m_\infty.

Lemma 3.3 yields $|m_t - m_\infty| \le \delta_t$. Then

\sup_a |\rho_t(a)-\rho_\infty(a)|
= \sup_a |(R_t(a)-m_t) - (R_\infty(a)-m_\infty)|
\le \delta_t + |m_t-m_\infty| \le 2\delta_t \to 0 \quad \text{a.s.}

Applying Lemma 3.3 to the maxima gives $|\rho_{\max,t} - \rho_{\max,\infty}| \le 2\delta_t \to 0$ a.s. Since $\rho_{\max,\infty} > 0$, there exists a deterministic $\eta>0$ such that $\rho_{\max,\infty} = 2\eta$. Because $\rho_{\max,t} \to \rho_{\max,\infty}$ a.s., we have

\mathbb{P}\bigl(\exists T\, \forall t\ge T: \rho_{\max,t} \ge \eta \bigr) = 1.

In particular, $\rho_{\max,t}>0$ for all large $t$ a.s., so the confidence $\mathcal{C}_t(a) = 1 - \rho_t(a)/\rho_{\max,t}$ is eventually well-defined. For those $t$, we may write

|\mathcal{C}_t(a) - \mathcal{C}_\infty(a)|
= \left| \frac{\rho_t(a)}{\rho_{\max,t}} - \frac{\rho_\infty(a)}{\rho_{\max,\infty}} \right|.

Now note that on the event $\rho_{\max,t}\ge \eta$, the function $(x,y)\mapsto x/y$ is Lipschitz on $[0,B] \times [\eta, B']$ (for appropriate bounds). More explicitly,

\left| \frac{x}{y} - \frac{x'}{y'} \right|
\le \frac{|x-x'|}{y} + \frac{x'|y-y'|}{yy'}.

Using boundedness of regrets by $B$, and $y,y'\ge \eta$, we get

|\mathcal{C}_t(a) - \mathcal{C}_\infty(a)|
\le \frac{|\rho_t(a)-\rho_\infty(a)|}{\eta} + \frac{B\,|\rho_{\max,t}-\rho_{\max,\infty}|}{\eta^2}.

Both numerator terms are bounded by $2\delta_t$ (for the first) and $2\delta_t$ (for the second), so the whole expression is $O(\delta_t) \to 0$ a.s. Hence $\mathcal{C}_t(a) \to \mathcal{C}_\infty(a)$ a.s. $\square$

---

Appendix D: Proof of the Discounted Consequence Functional Convergence (Theorem 4.2)

We give a detailed derivation of the absolute convergence bound.

Theorem 4.2. Assume $\|I(v)\|\le M$ for all $v\in V$, $\tau(v) \ge c \cdot \text{depth}(v)$ with $c>0$, and $\mu e^{-\lambda c} < 1$. Then the deterministic series

J(a_0) = \sum_{v\in V} P(v) \|I(v)\| e^{-\lambda \tau(v)}

converges absolutely, and $J(a_0) \le M/(1-\mu e^{-\lambda c})$. Moreover, the random sum $S = \sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}} \|I(v)\| e^{-\lambda\tau(v)}$ is finite a.s. and $\mathbb{E}[S] = J(a_0)$.

Proof.
For each depth $k\ge 0$, denote $V_k = \{v\in V : \text{depth}(v)=k\}$. The contribution of depth $k$ to $J(a_0)$ is

J_k := \sum_{v\in V_k} P(v)\, \|I(v)\|\, e^{-\lambda\tau(v)}.

Because $\tau(v)\ge c k$ and $\|I(v)\|\le M$, we have

J_k \le M e^{-\lambda c k} \sum_{v\in V_k} P(v).

The sum $\sum_{v\in V_k} P(v)$ is exactly the expected number of realised nodes at depth $k$, i.e., $\mathbb{E}[|V_k|]$. By Theorem 4.1, $\mathbb{E}[|V_k|] \le \mu^k$. Therefore,

J_k \le M (\mu e^{-\lambda c})^k.

Under the hypothesis $\mu e^{-\lambda c} < 1$, the geometric series $\sum_{k=0}^\infty (\mu e^{-\lambda c})^k$ converges to $1/(1-\mu e^{-\lambda c})$. Hence the series $\sum_{k} J_k$ converges absolutely, and $J(a_0) \le M/(1-\mu e^{-\lambda c})$.

Now consider the random total $S$. Since all terms are non‑negative, Tonelli’s theorem permits interchange of expectation and sum:

\mathbb{E}[S] = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}} \|I(v)\| e^{-\lambda\tau(v)} \right]
= \sum_{v\in V} \mathbb{E}[ \mathbf{1}_{\{v\in\mathcal{V}\}} \|I(v)\| e^{-\lambda\tau(v)} ].

For each $v$, the indicator $\mathbf{1}_{\{v\in\mathcal{V}\}}$ has expectation $P(v)$. Because $\|I(v)\|$ and $\tau(v)$ are marks that can depend on the realisation of $v$, we need to ensure the expectation factors appropriately. In the tree construction, marks at $v$ are assigned conditional on $v$ being realised. By the law of total expectation,

\mathbb{E}[ \mathbf{1}_{\{v\in\mathcal{V}\}} \|I(v)\| e^{-\lambda\tau(v)} ]
= P(v) \, \mathbb{E}[ \|I(v)\| e^{-\lambda\tau(v)} \mid v\in\mathcal{V} ].

However, the bound $\|I(v)\|\le M$ and $\tau(v)\ge c\cdot\text{depth}(v)$ hold almost surely conditional on realisation, so we still have

\mathbb{E}[ \mathbf{1}_{\{v\in\mathcal{V}\}} \|I(v)\| e^{-\lambda\tau(v)} ] \le P(v) M e^{-\lambda c \,\text{depth}(v)}.

Thus $\mathbb{E}[S] \le M/(1-\mu e^{-\lambda c}) < \infty$, implying $S<\infty$ a.s. $\square$

