Appendix F: Elaboration of the Fractal Dimension Argument (Theorem 4.3)

We give a slightly more detailed sketch of the proof of Theorem 4.3, assuming familiarity with iterated function systems (IFS) and Hausdorff dimension.

Theorem 4.3. Under the self‑similarity hypothesis (Definition 4.5), and assuming the open set condition, the Hausdorff dimension of the limit set of impact magnitudes (conditional on non‑extinction) is

D_b = \frac{\ln \mu}{\ln(1/r)}.

Elaboration.
Recall that each node $v$ generates exactly $\mu$ children (deterministic or almost surely), and for each child $v'$,

\|I(v')\| = r\,\|I(v)\|, \quad \tau(v') = \tau(v) + c.

Consider the set of all possible limit points of impact magnitudes along infinite paths that survive (non‑extinction). Starting from the root with impact magnitude $M_0 = \|I(\varnothing)\|$, the magnitudes after $k$ steps are of the form $M_0 r^k$. The branching structure creates a Cantor‑like set: the set of all infinite paths is a $\mu$-ary tree. Each path corresponds to a sequence of scaling factors.

The collection of all possible locations of impact magnitudes at generation $k$ is given by the IFS of $\mu$ contractive similarities on $\mathbb{R}$, each mapping $x \mapsto r x$ shifted appropriately (the shifts depend on the ordering of children but do not affect the dimension if they are separated enough). The attractor of this IFS is the set of all limit points of such constructions.

The similarity dimension $s$ solves $\mu \cdot r^s = 1$, giving $s = \ln \mu / \ln(1/r)$. Under the open set condition (i.e., the images of the similarities applied to a bounded open set are pairwise disjoint), the Hausdorff dimension of the attractor equals the similarity dimension (Falconer, Fractal Geometry, Theorem 9.3). The constant time increment $c$ separates branches temporally, but the fractal dimension refers purely to the spatial (impact magnitude) structure; the temporal coordinate does not affect the Hausdorff dimension of the projection.

Conditional on non‑extinction, with probability one, the limit set is the full attractor of the IFS, so its Hausdorff dimension is $D_b$. $\square$

